1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,040
Okay, welcome back.

2
00:00:01,340 --> 00:00:02,940
The next section is going to deal with

3
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:04,660
chronic obstructive pulmonary disease,

4
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:07,580
and the idea here is that these are chronic diseases

5
00:00:07,580 --> 00:00:09,880
that obviously affect the lung,

6
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:13,880
and that the key underlying premise in all of these diseases,

7
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:15,360
and there's going to be four total,

8
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,080
is that we have obstruction.

9
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:18,780
Now, what do we mean by obstruction?

10
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:22,620
It is an obstruction to getting air out of the lung.

11
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,960
That's the notion that we have to carry in the back of our mind

12
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:27,880
when we're thinking about these diseases.

13
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:30,920
In each of these disorders, there will be an obstruction

14
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:33,060
to getting air out of the lung.

15
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,900
Airway obstruction is the hallmark of these diseases,

16
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:38,620
as I've already highlighted to you,

17
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:40,560
and when you think about airway obstruction,

18
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:43,900
you should recognize that this airway obstruction in the patient

19
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:47,700
is clinically going to manifest with changes in spirometry.

20
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:51,120
Now, spirometry is the way by which we measure lung volumes,

21
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,180
and if you go back to normal physiology,

22
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:56,780
you'll recall that there are a series of measurements

23
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:58,780
that we can obtain from spirometry.

24
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:01,060
It's not the purpose of this discussion

25
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:03,180
to go into a detailed talk about spirometry,

26
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:04,640
but what I would like to highlight

27
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:06,640
are the key take-homes that you have to know,

28
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:08,880
especially in the context of disease,

29
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:12,860
from your understanding of spirometry.

30
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:15,380
So let's begin with one idea here,

31
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,780
and that is that if we've got airway obstruction,

32
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:20,020
and the problem is getting air out of the lung,

33
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:23,080
then the forced vital capacity will go down.

34
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:24,880
What's the forced vital capacity?

35
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:27,420
This is the amount of air that you can blow out

36
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:29,480
when you breathe all the way in

37
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:31,340
and then blow out as much as you can.

38
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:33,700
So it's a maximal expiration.

39
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:36,760
Otherwise stated, if you want to think about it another way,

40
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:39,180
if I asked you to blow out candles on a birthday cake,

41
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:40,660
you would use the FVC.

42
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:42,800
You would breathe in as deeply as possible

43
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:44,680
and then blow out as much as you could.

44
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:46,600
So in this particular set of diseases,

45
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:47,800
because there's obstruction,

46
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:48,880
the forced vital capacity,

47
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:51,600
the amount of air that you could blow out maximally,

48
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:52,640
is going to go down.

49
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:55,880
In addition, what's also going to go down is the FEV1.

50
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:58,820
The FEV1 is the forced expiratory volume,

51
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:00,880
and that's the amount of volume that you can get out

52
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,920
in a one-second period, hence the designation 1.

53
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:06,460
And so this represents the amount of air

54
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:08,860
that comes out of the lung with a forced expiration

55
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:11,980
in the very first second of that expiration.

56
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:14,160
This will also go down,

57
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,180
but it will go down more than the forced vital capacity.

58
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:19,360
And that's actually very important to understand,

59
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:21,880
because that translates in a key finding

60
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:23,500
that almost defines the disease,

61
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:24,900
which is that there is a decrease

62
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:27,620
in the FEV1 to FVC ratio.

63
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:29,720
It's probably easier to understand this

64
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:30,580
with a couple numbers,

65
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:32,180
so let's just plug in some numbers

66
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:33,880
to understand what I'm talking about.

67
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:37,300
Let me remind you that your normal forced vital capacity,

68
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:39,540
the normal amount of air that you could blow out

69
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:40,880
in a maximal expiration,

70
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:42,560
is about 5 liters.

71
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:44,060
That's the normal.

72
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:48,440
Now, of that 5 liters, about 4 liters

73
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:52,280
is what is removed within the first one second.

74
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:56,160
So that ratio, 4 over 5, is about 80%,

75
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:57,660
and that's the normal.

76
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:00,560
Now, let's say that a patient has COPD.

77
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:03,560
Well, obviously, this number is gonna go down,

78
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
and this number is really gonna go down,

79
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:07,800
and that's gonna change the percentage

80
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:10,300
so that the ratio decreases, for example.

81
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:11,880
In a patient with COPD,

82
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:14,620
we might expect that the forced vital capacity

83
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:16,680
goes down by one liter to four liters,

84
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:19,400
and we would expect that the FEV1

85
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:21,760
would significantly decrease from four liters

86
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:23,040
to maybe two liters,

87
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:25,740
and so now that ratio is 50%,

88
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:27,820
and so these patients have had a decrease

89
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:30,460
in their FEV1 to FVC ratio,

90
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:32,600
and that's a very important

91
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:36,260
high-yield physiology association for COPD.

92
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:38,000
It's very important to be aware of.

93
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:40,400
An additional finding in COPD

94
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:42,540
will be that the total lung capacity

95
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:43,380
will be increased.

96
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:44,760
The total lung capacity

97
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:46,300
is the total amount of air

98
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:48,500
that you can have within the lung

99
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:51,160
at the maximally,

100
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:53,060
and so that total lung capacity

101
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:54,200
is going to be increased

102
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,720
because there's gonna be air trapping.

103
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:57,220
If you can't get air out of the lung,

104
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,620
some air will be trapped in the lung,

105
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:00,160
and so the total amount of air

106
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:02,040
present within the lung maximally

107
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:03,180
will actually increase,

108
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:04,660
and that's the total lung capacity.

109
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:06,120
Generally speaking,

110
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:06,980
if you wanna know,

111
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:08,960
if you wanna think about this

112
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:09,720
with some numbers,

113
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:11,480
the normal total lung capacity

114
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:12,560
is about seven liters,

115
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:14,540
and in COPD,

116
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:16,200
it goes up to about eight liters.

117
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:18,320
So here's some very high-yield physiology

118
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:20,460
tie-in for this particular discussion.

119
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:22,920
Now, the very first of these diseases

120
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,460
is called chronic bronchitis.

121
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:26,980
Chronic bronchitis is interesting

122
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:28,500
because it's defined clinically.

123
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:30,180
Basically, the notion is

124
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:31,880
that the patient has a productive cough,

125
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:33,320
and that productive cough

126
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:34,780
lasts at least three months

127
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:36,720
over a minimum of two years.

128
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:38,740
This cough is so productive

129
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:40,120
that when the patient comes to you,

130
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:40,980
they will tell you

131
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:43,100
that they are coughing up cups of mucus,

132
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:44,500
tons and tons of mucus.

133
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:45,600
Some patients will come to you

134
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:47,700
saying they cough up buckets of mucus.

135
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:49,240
That's how much mucus

136
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:50,500
is produced in these patients.

137
00:04:51,020 --> 00:04:52,320
Now, the next question would be why.

138
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:53,500
What drives this disease?

139
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:56,080
Basically, it's a smoking-related disease.

140
00:04:56,540 --> 00:04:57,420
To understand this,

141
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:58,440
you actually have to understand

142
00:04:58,440 --> 00:04:59,780
a little bit of normal histology,

143
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:01,480
so let me take you back one step.

144
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:04,380
Here is the lumen of the bronchus,

145
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:05,660
and this is chronic bronchitis,

146
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:07,380
so it would involve large airways

147
00:05:07,380 --> 00:05:08,080
like the bronchus,

148
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:09,860
so this is the lumen of the bronchus,

149
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:11,360
and then you're well aware

150
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:14,700
that the epithelium that lines this region

151
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:16,480
is called respiratory epithelium,

152
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:18,680
and remember that respiratory epithelium

153
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:19,760
is pseudostratified,

154
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,460
which means that some nuclei are high

155
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:22,760
and some nuclei are low,

156
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:24,920
and it's called pseudostratified

157
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:25,940
because all the cells

158
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:27,560
really do touch the basement membrane.

159
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:29,700
So that's pseudostratified columnar,

160
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:30,580
the cells are tall,

161
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:32,580
ciliated, the cells have cilia,

162
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:34,040
epithelium,

163
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:34,760
and again,

164
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:35,560
all of these cells

165
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:36,640
sit on a basement membrane,

166
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:38,720
and that's the epithelium.

167
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:39,860
Underneath that epithelium

168
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:40,940
is a lamina propria,

169
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:42,040
and recall that

170
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:42,900
in the lamina propria,

171
00:05:43,020 --> 00:05:44,660
you have very large caliber

172
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:45,820
blood vessels,

173
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:46,860
venules basically,

174
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:48,020
and these venules,

175
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:49,260
they bring warm blood

176
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:50,100
into the region,

177
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:51,520
which then allows the air

178
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:52,180
to be warmed

179
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:53,160
before it goes down

180
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:54,220
to the bottom of the lung

181
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:54,920
for exchange,

182
00:05:55,460 --> 00:05:56,740
and so that's the lamina propria,

183
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:57,380
and then underneath

184
00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:58,200
the lamina propria,

185
00:05:58,300 --> 00:05:58,980
you have something called

186
00:05:58,980 --> 00:05:59,820
the submucosa,

187
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:00,860
and remember that

188
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:02,440
the submucosa contains glands

189
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:03,720
and there's two types of glands.

190
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:04,980
One gland is called

191
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:05,800
the serous gland

192
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:06,800
and that serous gland

193
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:08,080
produces watery fluid

194
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,000
that helps to humidify the air.

195
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:11,080
The watery fluid

196
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:11,840
goes up here

197
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:13,260
and then humidifies the air

198
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:14,140
before it goes down

199
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:14,660
into the lung,

200
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:15,460
and of course,

201
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:16,880
the purpose of humidifying the air

202
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:17,600
is that you don't want

203
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:18,340
to turn the lung

204
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:19,420
into a dry sponge.

205
00:06:19,980 --> 00:06:20,880
At the same time,

206
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:22,000
there are mucinous glands

207
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:22,900
and the mucinous glands

208
00:06:22,900 --> 00:06:24,200
produce mucus-like fluid,

209
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:25,320
which then goes

210
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:26,980
and lines the epithelial surface,

211
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:28,360
and that mucusy fluid

212
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:29,540
acts as a filter

213
00:06:29,540 --> 00:06:30,200
for particles

214
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:31,340
as you're breathing in

215
00:06:31,340 --> 00:06:32,120
because you don't want

216
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:33,180
the pollution and junk

217
00:06:33,180 --> 00:06:34,160
from the outside ear

218
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:35,100
to actually come down

219
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:35,640
into the lung.

220
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:36,840
So that's the purpose

221
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:37,780
of the mucinous glands.

222
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:38,960
And then, of course,

223
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:40,060
underneath the submucosa

224
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:41,360
in a large airway

225
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:42,020
like the bronchus

226
00:06:42,020 --> 00:06:42,720
would be cartilage.

227
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:44,960
Now, let me just remind you

228
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:46,300
that normally,

229
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:49,020
the ratio of these glands here

230
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:50,920
to the entire thickness

231
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:51,580
of the wall

232
00:06:51,580 --> 00:06:53,460
is less than 40%,

233
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:54,980
i.e.,

234
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:55,520
basically,

235
00:06:55,720 --> 00:06:56,720
the mucinous glands

236
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:58,840
take up about 40%

237
00:06:58,840 --> 00:06:59,500
or less

238
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:00,100
of the thickness

239
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:00,640
of the wall.

240
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:01,720
Now, let's imagine

241
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:02,340
that there's a patient

242
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:03,000
that smokes.

243
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:03,740
If they smoke,

244
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:04,500
they're going to be bringing

245
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,380
in a ton of pollutants,

246
00:07:06,460 --> 00:07:07,960
a ton of foreign particles

247
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:08,680
into their lung.

248
00:07:09,140 --> 00:07:09,820
And in response,

249
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:10,480
the lung is going

250
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:11,520
to try to respond

251
00:07:11,520 --> 00:07:12,720
to deal with all

252
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:14,280
that foreign material

253
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:15,260
by increasing

254
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:16,500
the amount of mucus produced.

255
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:17,400
So if you need

256
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:17,840
to increase

257
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:18,820
the amount of mucus produced,

258
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:19,560
you're going to undergo

259
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:20,440
hyperplasia

260
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:22,000
and hypertrophy

261
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,420
of these mucinous glands.

262
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:24,960
So as we get

263
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:26,420
excess mucinous glands

264
00:07:26,420 --> 00:07:27,920
because of the smoking,

265
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:29,720
then these excess

266
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:30,440
mucinous glands

267
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:31,900
are going to take over,

268
00:07:32,700 --> 00:07:33,540
why don't we say this,

269
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:34,580
the excess mucinous glands

270
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:35,320
are going to increase

271
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:35,880
in thickness

272
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:37,200
relative to the overall

273
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:38,140
thickness of the wall.

274
00:07:38,620 --> 00:07:39,640
And so the mucinous glands,

275
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:40,260
their thickness

276
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:41,120
will become greater

277
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:43,060
than 50%

278
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:44,500
of the overall wall.

279
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:45,580
And that's important

280
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:46,600
because that's called

281
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:48,800
the read index.

282
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:50,560
The read index measures

283
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:51,060
the thickness

284
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:52,000
of the mucinous glands

285
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:53,680
relative to the entire

286
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:54,580
thickness of the wall.

287
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:56,340
And in chronic bronchitis,

288
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:57,440
it becomes greater

289
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:58,360
than 50%.

290
00:07:58,360 --> 00:07:59,800
Now what's also important

291
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:00,340
to note is that

292
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:01,020
if we've got all

293
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:01,880
of these mucinous glands

294
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:02,560
and they're producing

295
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:03,280
a ton of mucus,

296
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:04,220
guess where all that mucus

297
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:04,840
is going to go?

298
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:05,620
Into the lumen.

299
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:06,920
And guess how the patient

300
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:07,700
is going to get rid of it?

301
00:08:07,900 --> 00:08:08,560
Well some of it

302
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:09,600
is going to be coughed up

303
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:10,400
which is then going to

304
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:11,900
create the chronic bronchitis

305
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:14,000
with the productive cough

306
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:14,880
that lasts at least

307
00:08:14,880 --> 00:08:15,400
three months

308
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:16,060
over a minimum

309
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:17,840
of two years.

310
00:08:18,220 --> 00:08:19,180
And at the same time,

311
00:08:19,580 --> 00:08:20,240
some of the mucus

312
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:21,340
is also going to go back

313
00:08:21,340 --> 00:08:21,820
and it's going to

314
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:22,660
plug the airways.

315
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:23,240
And when it plugs

316
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:23,800
the airways,

317
00:08:23,860 --> 00:08:24,800
there's going to be trapping

318
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:25,660
and there's going to be

319
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:26,320
an obstruction

320
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:27,360
to getting air

321
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:28,040
out of the lung.

322
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:29,740
With all of that background,

323
00:08:29,940 --> 00:08:30,740
now you can understand

324
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:31,260
the disease.

325
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:33,060
So what did I say?

326
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:34,020
I told you that it is

327
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:34,800
highly associated

328
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:35,380
with smoking.

329
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:36,680
That should make sense.

330
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:37,940
At the same time,

331
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,040
I tell you in your text,

332
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:39,540
and go back

333
00:08:39,540 --> 00:08:40,420
and read the text now,

334
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:41,540
there's hypertrophy

335
00:08:41,540 --> 00:08:43,120
of the bronchial mucinous glands.

336
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:44,820
This increases the thickness

337
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:46,140
of the mucus glands

338
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:47,680
relative to the overall

339
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:48,680
bronchial wall thickness

340
00:08:48,680 --> 00:08:50,220
and the read index

341
00:08:50,220 --> 00:08:51,840
becomes greater than 50%.

342
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:52,760
Again, normally

343
00:08:52,760 --> 00:08:54,180
it should be less than 40%.

344
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:55,600
Let's illustrate that again

345
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:57,240
with a real histologic image.

346
00:08:57,880 --> 00:08:58,920
What I've tried to do

347
00:08:58,920 --> 00:08:59,420
in this image

348
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:00,340
is I've tried to capture

349
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:01,500
the entire wall thickness.

350
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:03,020
So we start up here

351
00:09:03,020 --> 00:09:03,960
at the very top.

352
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:05,700
That's the respiratory epithelium

353
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:06,600
in the upper right-hand

354
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:08,060
side of this slide.

355
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:09,180
This is sitting

356
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:10,100
on a basement membrane.

357
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:11,520
Underneath the basement membrane

358
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:12,660
is a lamina propria

359
00:09:12,660 --> 00:09:14,840
which contains dilated venules

360
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:16,100
which basically bring in

361
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:16,700
warm blood

362
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:18,220
in order to humidify the air.

363
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:19,880
These are the mucinous glands

364
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:20,620
from here

365
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:21,540
all the way here.

366
00:09:21,540 --> 00:09:22,160
You can see

367
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:23,520
all these little circular spaces

368
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:24,320
that are lined

369
00:09:24,320 --> 00:09:25,360
by mucinous cells

370
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:26,480
and these cells

371
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:27,620
basically produce mucus

372
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:28,580
which goes up

373
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:30,040
and then goes out

374
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:31,280
into the luminal space.

375
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:33,960
Now, it's important to note

376
00:09:33,960 --> 00:09:35,520
that normally

377
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:36,100
the thickness

378
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:37,640
of these mucinous glands

379
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:38,400
should be about

380
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:40,440
40% or less

381
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:41,700
of the entire wall thickness.

382
00:09:42,140 --> 00:09:43,340
Here's the cartilage down here.

383
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:44,400
If I look at this

384
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:45,540
entire wall thickness

385
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:46,080
up here

386
00:09:46,080 --> 00:09:47,520
and I ask you

387
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:48,200
a simple question.

388
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:49,200
What percentage

389
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:49,880
of that thickness

390
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:50,580
is taken over

391
00:09:50,580 --> 00:09:51,760
by the mucinous glands

392
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:52,880
I think you would agree

393
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:53,220
with me

394
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:53,940
that it's certainly

395
00:09:53,940 --> 00:09:55,060
greater than 50%

396
00:09:55,060 --> 00:09:56,120
and so

397
00:09:56,120 --> 00:09:57,420
greater than 50%

398
00:09:57,420 --> 00:09:58,760
thickness of the mucinous glands

399
00:09:58,760 --> 00:09:59,940
is an increase

400
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:00,800
in the read index

401
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:01,820
which is one of the

402
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:02,720
classic findings

403
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:03,880
in chronic bronchitis.

404
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:05,380
Now, these patients

405
00:10:05,380 --> 00:10:06,540
with chronic bronchitis

406
00:10:06,540 --> 00:10:07,180
are going to present

407
00:10:07,180 --> 00:10:08,220
with a productive cough

408
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:09,100
but that makes sense

409
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:09,960
because they're making

410
00:10:09,960 --> 00:10:10,780
a ton of mucus

411
00:10:10,780 --> 00:10:12,000
and that ton of mucus

412
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:12,760
is going to come out

413
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:13,340
as a cough.

414
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:14,460
So that's the

415
00:10:14,460 --> 00:10:16,020
classic clinical feature

416
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:16,520
and there's no

417
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:17,480
rocket science here.

418
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:19,100
Another important finding

419
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:19,680
is that they're going

420
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:20,400
to get an increase

421
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:21,600
in the PaCO2

422
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:22,480
and a decrease

423
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:23,640
in the PaO2

424
00:10:23,640 --> 00:10:24,560
and that's going

425
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:25,760
to present a cyanosis

426
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:27,420
or blue discoloration

427
00:10:27,420 --> 00:10:28,720
in the patient.

428
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:31,320
Now, in order to understand

429
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:32,140
that we can probably

430
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:33,020
go back a step

431
00:10:33,020 --> 00:10:33,740
and remind ourselves

432
00:10:33,740 --> 00:10:34,560
that the goal

433
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:35,100
of the lung

434
00:10:35,100 --> 00:10:36,140
is to generate

435
00:10:36,140 --> 00:10:38,180
a partial pressure

436
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:39,040
of oxygen

437
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:41,620
in this alveolar airspace

438
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:42,640
of P big AO2

439
00:10:42,640 --> 00:10:44,200
and that P big AO2

440
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:45,100
produces a pressure

441
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:46,360
that squeezes oxygen

442
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:48,100
into the blood vessels

443
00:10:48,100 --> 00:10:48,880
and when it squeezes

444
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:50,100
oxygen into the blood vessels

445
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:52,660
that creates a P small aO2

446
00:10:52,660 --> 00:10:53,860
within these blood vessels.

447
00:10:54,620 --> 00:10:56,200
So, that's the normal.

448
00:10:56,740 --> 00:10:57,380
Now, of course,

449
00:10:57,520 --> 00:10:58,460
you don't want to have

450
00:10:58,460 --> 00:10:59,280
carbon dioxide

451
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:00,840
or as soon as you pick up

452
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:01,640
any carbon dioxide

453
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:02,600
in the alveolar space

454
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:03,640
you don't want to keep it there

455
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:04,560
you want to blow it out

456
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:07,480
because there is a limit

457
00:11:07,480 --> 00:11:08,400
to the amount of pressure

458
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:09,240
that you can generate

459
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:10,220
within this region

460
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:11,460
and you want all

461
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:12,060
of that pressure

462
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:13,760
to be pressure of oxygen.

463
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:15,440
If you start putting

464
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:16,820
carbon dioxide here

465
00:11:16,820 --> 00:11:17,920
so that you now generate

466
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,440
a P ACO2

467
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:20,640
that's significant

468
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:22,680
that increase in P ACO2

469
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:23,800
will automatically

470
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:25,340
decrease the P AO2

471
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:26,460
and that decrease

472
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:27,300
in P AO2

473
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:28,180
will mean that there's

474
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:28,880
less pressure

475
00:11:28,880 --> 00:11:30,440
to squeeze the oxygen

476
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:32,200
across that barrier

477
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:33,900
into the blood

478
00:11:33,900 --> 00:11:36,040
and so the P small aO2

479
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:37,160
will also decrease.

480
00:11:37,480 --> 00:11:38,900
And so basically

481
00:11:38,900 --> 00:11:39,920
this is the summary

482
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:41,460
of what's going to happen

483
00:11:41,460 --> 00:11:42,060
lab-wise.

484
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:43,380
The P big ACO2

485
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:43,960
will go up

486
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:45,980
along with the P small aCO2

487
00:11:45,980 --> 00:11:49,020
and the P big AO2

488
00:11:49,020 --> 00:11:50,280
will go down

489
00:11:50,280 --> 00:11:52,640
along with the P small aO2.

490
00:11:53,220 --> 00:11:54,120
So that's the summary

491
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:55,740
of how the patients

492
00:11:55,740 --> 00:11:56,400
become cyanotic

493
00:11:57,020 --> 00:11:57,920
and why they get

494
00:11:57,920 --> 00:11:59,900
these particular lab findings.

495
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:01,560
Another important thing

496
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:02,340
to be familiar with

497
00:12:02,340 --> 00:12:03,180
is that there's an increased

498
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:03,940
risk of infection.

499
00:12:04,340 --> 00:12:04,900
Here's a general

500
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:05,840
pathology principle.

501
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:06,800
Anytime you have a tube

502
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:07,460
and you block it

503
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:08,060
you're going to get

504
00:12:08,060 --> 00:12:09,060
an infection behind

505
00:12:09,060 --> 00:12:09,620
that block.

506
00:12:10,100 --> 00:12:10,740
So for example

507
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:11,920
patients who have BPH

508
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:12,960
they're not able

509
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:13,800
to get urine out

510
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:14,640
very well

511
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:15,960
and so the urine piles up

512
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:16,720
in the bladder

513
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:17,520
and there's an increased

514
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:18,620
risk of bladder infection

515
00:12:18,620 --> 00:12:19,420
in those patients.

516
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:20,600
Similarly here

517
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:21,800
if you've got airways

518
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:23,080
and you can imagine

519
00:12:23,080 --> 00:12:23,780
that the airways

520
00:12:23,780 --> 00:12:24,520
as they branch

521
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:25,540
if there's mucus plugging

522
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:26,540
you're going to get

523
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:28,300
infection behind that block

524
00:12:28,300 --> 00:12:29,560
and so there's an increased

525
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:30,300
risk of infection

526
00:12:30,300 --> 00:12:31,060
in these patients.

527
00:12:31,060 --> 00:12:34,040
And the final thing

528
00:12:34,040 --> 00:12:34,700
to be aware of

529
00:12:34,700 --> 00:12:35,560
is that there's a risk

530
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:36,440
of core pulmonale

531
00:12:36,440 --> 00:12:37,360
in these patients.

532
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:38,780
So what's core pulmonale?

533
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:39,880
That requires a little bit

534
00:12:39,880 --> 00:12:40,840
of understanding

535
00:12:40,840 --> 00:12:41,540
of physiology.

536
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:42,700
Now recall that

537
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:43,320
the blood vessels

538
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:43,960
in the lung

539
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:45,260
they are designed

540
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:46,560
to recognize

541
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:47,600
that if there is

542
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:48,700
a lack of oxygen

543
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:49,940
in a particular region

544
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:50,480
of the lung

545
00:12:50,480 --> 00:12:51,960
that they should actually

546
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:52,880
clamp down

547
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:53,600
to shut down

548
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:54,100
that region

549
00:12:54,100 --> 00:12:54,620
and instead

550
00:12:54,620 --> 00:12:55,320
shunt the blood

551
00:12:55,320 --> 00:12:56,240
in another direction.

552
00:12:56,780 --> 00:12:57,240
Let's just take

553
00:12:57,240 --> 00:12:57,900
a simple example.

554
00:12:58,020 --> 00:12:58,600
Let's say a patient

555
00:12:58,600 --> 00:12:59,220
has pneumonia.

556
00:12:59,720 --> 00:13:01,960
It's in the left upper lobe.

557
00:13:02,260 --> 00:13:02,700
If they've got

558
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:03,720
a left upper lobe pneumonia

559
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:04,700
there's really not much

560
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:05,680
oxygen getting in there

561
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:06,600
because the lung

562
00:13:06,600 --> 00:13:07,440
is basically loaded

563
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:07,980
with pus

564
00:13:07,980 --> 00:13:09,040
and inflammatory debris.

565
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:10,280
So in response

566
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:11,760
what the capillaries

567
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:13,140
of the alveolar air sacs do

568
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:14,080
actually this happens

569
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:14,460
at the level

570
00:13:14,460 --> 00:13:15,340
of the arterioles

571
00:13:15,340 --> 00:13:16,560
but what the arterioles

572
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:18,060
do is they squeeze down

573
00:13:18,060 --> 00:13:19,260
to decrease the amount

574
00:13:19,260 --> 00:13:19,860
of blood going

575
00:13:19,860 --> 00:13:21,080
to the left upper lobe

576
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:21,880
and instead

577
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:22,840
blood will be shunted

578
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:23,720
to other parts

579
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:24,220
of the lung

580
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:25,100
with the hope

581
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:25,820
that that blood

582
00:13:25,820 --> 00:13:26,280
will be able

583
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:27,140
to meet oxygen

584
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:27,880
and to be able

585
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:28,400
to carry out

586
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:29,280
the exchange

587
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:30,560
that is the purpose

588
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:31,140
of the lung.

589
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:32,780
So that makes sense

590
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:33,560
and that's very good

591
00:13:33,560 --> 00:13:34,600
because if I've got

592
00:13:34,600 --> 00:13:35,580
an upper lobe pneumonia

593
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:36,460
in the left lobe

594
00:13:36,460 --> 00:13:37,980
I don't want my blood

595
00:13:37,980 --> 00:13:38,540
going there

596
00:13:38,540 --> 00:13:39,580
looking for oxygen

597
00:13:39,580 --> 00:13:40,100
and instead

598
00:13:40,100 --> 00:13:41,020
I'd like to send it

599
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:41,680
